Notebook[{
Cell["DEFINING FUNCTIONS", "Title"],

Cell["Simple One-Line Function Definition", "Subtitle"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[x_]\  := \ x^2\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The above line defines a function that takes the square of its argument.   x_ \
(the symbol called x with an underscore) is called a pattern (which \"match\" \
anything), and := is called delayed assignment, different from assignment =.  \
We use the function like this:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(4\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[a]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\^2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f[1 + x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 + x)\)\^2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["This function takes any argument, including strings:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f["\<this\>"]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("this"\^2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Assignment = and Delayed Assignment :=", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
= (or Set[ ])  : the right-hand side expression is evaluated, and result sets \
to the variable (expression) on the left-hand side.
:= (or SetDelayed[ ]) : the right-hand side expression without evaluation is \
set to the variable (expression) on the left-hand side.
 \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\  = \ 3; \ x\  = \ a + 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(4\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?x\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("Global`x"\)], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[
      StyleBox[\(x\  = \ 4\),
        ShowStringCharacters->True,
        NumberMarks->True],
      InputForm[ 
        Definition[ x]],
      Editable->True,
      AutoDelete->True]], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
With Set command \"=\", we associate number 4 with the variable x, since a + \
1 has been evaluated to 4 before set the value for x.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a\  = \ 3; \ x\  := \ a\  + \ 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?x\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \("Global`x"\)], "Print"],

Cell[BoxData[
    InterpretationBox[
      StyleBox[\(x\  := \ a\  + \ 1\),
        ShowStringCharacters->True,
        NumberMarks->True],
      InputForm[ 
        Definition[ x]],
      Editable->True,
      AutoDelete->True]], "Print"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
With SetDelayed :=, we associated a + 1 with x, the expression a+1 is delayed \
for evaluation. 
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["More on Function Definition", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
If we like to have a function that takes only integer, we can say:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g[x_Integer]\  := \ x^2\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({g[3], \ g[x], \ g[3.5]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"9", ",", \(g[x]\), ",", 
        RowBox[{"g", "[", 
          StyleBox["3.5`",
            StyleBoxAutoDelete->True,
            PrintPrecision->2], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["We can define a function with any number of arguments:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[a_, \ b_, \ c_]\  := \ \((a + b + c)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h[1, 2, q]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3 + q\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["You can define function with a variable number of arguments:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(v[a__]\  := \ Plus[a]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({v[1], \ v[1, 3], \ v[1, 2, 3, 4]}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Function Definition with More than One-Line of Code: programs\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
If we define a function with slightly complicated code of more than one line, \
we can used the Module[ ] to group the code as a single unit.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\(prog[
        x_]\)\(\ \)\(:=\)\(\ \)\(Module[\ {a, b, c}, \ \n\t\t\ta\  = \ 
          1; \n\t\t\tb\  = \ 2; \n\t\t\tc\  = \ a\  + \ b; \n\t\t\tx\  + \ 
          c]\)\(\t\t\)\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Note: The variables in the first list {a,b,c} specifies LOCAL variables to \
this function. Statements are separated by semicolon \";\".   The return \
value of the function is last expression, x + c, without an ending semicolon. \
 The argument x must be treated as a constant, rather than a variable (i.e., \
you cannot do, e.g., x = a + b).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(prog[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(6\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Patterns", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Pattern is an important concept in ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "'s syntax:  f[x_] is a pattern.  Patterns are used for matching an \
expression.  So f[x_] matched any expression with an explicit f, followed by \
square brackets with x_ being replaced by any expression.  So f[x_] matches \
f[1], f[x], or f[\"this\"], but not g[2].   If pattern matches, the \
definition associated with that pattern is executed.   In the above example, \
f[something] is replaced by something^2.  \n\ng[x_Integer]  matches a \
function g with integer argument, so, it matches g[2], but not g[a] (assuming \
we did not say a = 3, so a is not an integer).  Unmatched patterns are not \
executed and returned the same as input.   h[a_, b_, c_] matches function h \
with three arbitrary arguments.\n\nv[a__] (two underscores) matches function \
v with 1 or more arguments.  So it matches v[1], v[1,2],  or v[a,b,c].\n\nYou \
can test if pattern pattn match expression expr by the command:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatchQ[expr, \ pattn]\)], "Input"],

Cell["For example,", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MatchQ[f[y], \ f[x_]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "More pattern types are available, see the quick reference sheet, and \
Chapter 2.3. of the ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " book of S. Wolfram.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["Substitution Operations", "Title"],

Cell["\<\
A convenient way for evaluation an expression is by variable substitution. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 + x)\)^5\ \ \  /. \ x -> \ 3\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1024\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
While x = 3 globally sets x to 3, x -> 3 replaces x by 3 only locally at the \
current expression. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Sin[x], Cos[x], Exp[x]} /. x -> 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({0, 1, 1}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[x^2\  + \ 3  x\  - 1\  == \ 0, x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{x \[Rule] 1\/2\ \((\(-3\) - \@13)\)}, {x \[Rule] 
          1\/2\ \((\(-3\) + \@13)\)}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  /. \ %19\  // \ N\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({\(-3.30277563773199478`\), 0.302775637731994562`}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Expression of the form x -> 1.2 is called a rule, and output from solving an \
equation gives you a list of rules.  \
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Prefix, postfix, and infix notation 
for function application.\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell["We normally say Sin[x], but we could say:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Sin\ @\ Pi\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Pi\  // \ Sin\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(0\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c}\ ~Join~\ {d, f}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a, b, c, d, f}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\

READINGS:\
\>", "Title"],

Cell["\<\
On function definitions:  Sec. 1.7
On patterns: Sec. 2.3\
\>", "Subtitle"]
},
FrontEndVersion->"4.1 for Microsoft Windows",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1024}, {0, 695}},
WindowSize->{1016, 668},
WindowMargins->{{0, Automatic}, {Automatic, 0}},
Magnification->3,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]

